1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,023
Good afternoon. Today we are presenting the Library Usage Monitoring and Statistics System.

2
00:00:06,023 --> 00:00:12,443
A web application built to record, monitor, and analyse how our college library is actually used.

3
00:00:12,443 --> 00:00:18,069
Over the next few minutes we will walk through the problem that started this project,

4
00:00:18,069 --> 00:00:24,820
the objectives we set, the technology behind the system, and how students and staff use it day to day.

5
00:00:24,820 --> 00:00:28,445
Traditionally, library attendance is recorded by hand.

6
00:00:28,445 --> 00:00:35,427
A student signs a paper logbook on the way in, and staff copy those entries later if a report is needed.

7
00:00:35,427 --> 00:00:38,381
That approach works, but it has real limits.

8
00:00:38,381 --> 00:00:44,222
Handwriting can be difficult to read, and entries can be skipped when the desk is busy.

9
00:00:44,222 --> 00:00:50,130
Nobody can answer a simple question like how many students are in the library right now,

10
00:00:50,130 --> 00:00:52,547
without walking around and counting.

11
00:00:52,547 --> 00:00:56,374
Totals for a whole semester mean adding up pages by hand.

12
00:00:56,374 --> 00:01:00,670
Our project replaces that logbook with a centralised web system,

13
00:01:00,670 --> 00:01:04,900
so that every entry and exit is recorded the moment it happens.

14
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:07,621
We set three objectives for this system.

15
00:01:07,621 --> 00:01:11,090
The first is to develop the web application itself,

16
00:01:11,090 --> 00:01:16,872
a single place where students record their visits and where staff manage the library.

17
00:01:16,872 --> 00:01:22,994
Because it runs in a browser, students use it on their own phones with nothing to install.

18
00:01:22,994 --> 00:01:26,463
The second objective is systematic data management.

19
00:01:26,463 --> 00:01:31,973
Every member record and every visit is stored in a database rather than on paper,

20
00:01:31,973 --> 00:01:36,122
so information can be searched, verified, and reviewed later.

21
00:01:36,122 --> 00:01:41,632
Staff can filter records by department, by academic year, or by a range of dates,

22
00:01:41,632 --> 00:01:43,673
and get an answer immediately.

23
00:01:43,673 --> 00:01:46,530
The third objective covers administration.

24
00:01:46,530 --> 00:01:52,652
The system gives library staff a dashboard showing current occupancy and usage statistics,

25
00:01:52,652 --> 00:01:55,781
and it generates reports from the stored data,

26
00:01:55,781 --> 00:02:00,270
which can be printed as PDF, or downloaded as CSV and Excel files.

27
00:02:00,270 --> 00:02:04,154
Let me explain how the system is built, layer by layer.

28
00:02:04,154 --> 00:02:11,498
What the user sees is built with HTML5 and Tailwind CSS, with plain JavaScript handling the interaction.

29
00:02:11,498 --> 00:02:17,359
The layout is responsive, so the same pages work on a phone at the library entrance

30
00:02:17,359 --> 00:02:20,043
and on a desktop at the staff counter.

31
00:02:20,043 --> 00:02:26,893
Behind that, the application runs on PHP 8 on an Apache server, using a front controller pattern.

32
00:02:26,893 --> 00:02:29,859
Every request enters through a single file

33
00:02:29,859 --> 00:02:34,307
that decides which page or which API endpoint should handle it.

34
00:02:34,307 --> 00:02:38,686
This is also the layer that checks whether a user is logged in

35
00:02:38,686 --> 00:02:42,993
and whether they are allowed to see what they are asking for.

36
00:02:42,993 --> 00:02:47,230
The data sits in MySQL, in a database named library_checkin.

37
00:02:47,230 --> 00:02:51,891
All access goes through PHP Data Objects with prepared statements,

38
00:02:51,891 --> 00:02:56,481
which keeps the values a user types separate from the SQL itself,

39
00:02:56,481 --> 00:02:59,588
a standard protection against SQL injection.

40
00:02:59,588 --> 00:03:03,967
Finally, printable reports are produced with the mPDF library,

41
00:03:03,967 --> 00:03:07,780
together with the PHP GD extension for image handling.

42
00:03:07,780 --> 00:03:11,075
Now let us follow a student through the system.

43
00:03:11,075 --> 00:03:17,384
A student opens the library portal in any browser, usually on their phone as they walk in.

44
00:03:17,384 --> 00:03:20,749
They sign in with their student ID and password.

45
00:03:20,749 --> 00:03:26,218
If it is their first visit, they can register their profile in the same place:

46
00:03:26,218 --> 00:03:28,601
name, department, level, and year.

47
00:03:28,601 --> 00:03:33,439
Once signed in, the main screen shows a single large check-in button.

48
00:03:33,439 --> 00:03:39,117
The student presses it, chooses roughly how long they plan to stay, and confirms.

49
00:03:39,117 --> 00:03:42,973
The system records the exact time and stores the visit.

50
00:03:42,973 --> 00:03:47,740
If the same student presses check-in again while already checked in,

51
00:03:47,740 --> 00:03:50,895
the system will not create a duplicate entry.

52
00:03:50,895 --> 00:03:55,311
It knows they are already inside, and offers check-out instead.

53
00:03:55,311 --> 00:04:01,060
When they leave, they press the same button to check out, and the visit is closed.

54
00:04:01,060 --> 00:04:03,487
Those steps are simple on the surface,

55
00:04:03,487 --> 00:04:08,341
but several features work together behind them to keep the records reliable.

56
00:04:08,341 --> 00:04:11,215
The system manages how long each visit lasts,

57
00:04:11,215 --> 00:04:14,664
separates what students may do from what staff may do,

58
00:04:14,664 --> 00:04:17,538
keeps a live view of who is currently inside,

59
00:04:17,538 --> 00:04:23,158
prevents duplicate or unfinished entries, and preserves the full history of every visit.

60
00:04:23,158 --> 00:04:27,820
The next few sections look at the most important of these in more detail.

61
00:04:27,820 --> 00:04:33,475
When a student checks in, they choose a planned duration, for example, two hours.

62
00:04:33,475 --> 00:04:38,083
The system will not accept a time that runs past the closing hour,

63
00:04:38,083 --> 00:04:41,713
so a planned visit always ends within opening times.

64
00:04:41,713 --> 00:04:47,019
If they need longer, they can extend the session by thirty or sixty minutes,

65
00:04:47,019 --> 00:04:48,904
again within opening hours.

66
00:04:48,904 --> 00:04:52,953
The most useful part is what happens when someone forgets.

67
00:04:52,953 --> 00:04:57,142
In a paper logbook, a student who leaves without signing out

68
00:04:57,142 --> 00:04:59,865
simply leaves an open, unfinished line.

69
00:04:59,865 --> 00:05:04,333
Here, the system closes the visit automatically at closing time.

70
00:05:04,333 --> 00:05:09,360
That means the history contains complete visits with a start and an end,

71
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:12,990
and the average duration statistics stay meaningful.

72
00:05:12,990 --> 00:05:18,049
For library staff, the handwritten logbook is replaced by database records.

73
00:05:18,049 --> 00:05:23,108
Staff can see current occupancy at any moment, open any attendance history,

74
00:05:23,108 --> 00:05:25,671
and compare usage between departments.

75
00:05:25,671 --> 00:05:30,190
The system also records failed login attempts in a dedicated table,

76
00:05:30,190 --> 00:05:32,146
which supports rate limiting.

77
00:05:32,146 --> 00:05:37,947
Repeated wrong passwords are slowed down rather than allowed to continue indefinitely.

78
00:05:37,947 --> 00:05:42,533
Changes to the database structure are tracked in a migrations table,

79
00:05:42,533 --> 00:05:45,838
so the schema can be updated in a controlled way.

80
00:05:45,838 --> 00:05:50,155
The figure shown here, one hundred percent digital traceability,

81
00:05:50,155 --> 00:05:52,988
describes the design goal of this project:

82
00:05:52,988 --> 00:05:57,844
that every recorded visit exists as a digital record that can be traced,

83
00:05:57,844 --> 00:06:00,340
rather than as a line of handwriting.

84
00:06:00,340 --> 00:06:03,377
Because every visit is stored with a timestamp,

85
00:06:03,377 --> 00:06:08,610
the dashboard can group them and show when the library is busiest across the day:

86
00:06:08,610 --> 00:06:11,194
morning, midday, afternoon, and evening.

87
00:06:11,194 --> 00:06:13,908
The values on this slide are illustrative,

88
00:06:13,908 --> 00:06:19,787
shown to demonstrate how the chart presents a busy midday period against a quieter evening.

89
00:06:19,787 --> 00:06:21,596
The point is the capability.

90
00:06:21,596 --> 00:06:26,054
Once real attendance data accumulates, staff can see genuine patterns

91
00:06:26,054 --> 00:06:31,610
and use them when planning seating or deciding when more staff are needed at the desk.

92
00:06:31,610 --> 00:06:34,168
Not everyone sees the same system.

93
00:06:34,168 --> 00:06:40,412
Students can check in and out, and view their own attendance history, nothing more.

94
00:06:40,412 --> 00:06:45,829
They cannot open the dashboard, browse other records, or export reports.

95
00:06:45,829 --> 00:06:51,772
Library staff have full access: the dashboard and analytics, member management,

96
00:06:51,772 --> 00:06:57,339
the complete attendance history, and report export in PDF, CSV, and Excel.

97
00:06:57,339 --> 00:07:00,047
The login page is the same for both.

98
00:07:00,047 --> 00:07:03,734
The system reads the role attached to the account

99
00:07:03,734 --> 00:07:07,420
and decides what that person is allowed to reach.

100
00:07:07,420 --> 00:07:09,969
We built the system in four phases.

101
00:07:09,969 --> 00:07:16,377
Phase one was the database: designing the tables for students, users, and check-in logs,

102
00:07:16,377 --> 00:07:21,402
and setting up migrations so the structure could change safely later.

103
00:07:21,402 --> 00:07:27,664
Phase two was the core web application: the PHP backend, session-based authentication,

104
00:07:27,664 --> 00:07:31,014
the check-in flow, and the Tailwind interface.

105
00:07:31,014 --> 00:07:36,621
Phase three added the administrative side: the dashboard, mPDF report export,

106
00:07:36,621 --> 00:07:38,588
and rate limiting on login.

107
00:07:38,588 --> 00:07:43,539
Phase four was testing and deployment. We tested locally with XAMPP,

108
00:07:43,539 --> 00:07:48,054
and deployment to the live server runs through GitHub Actions,

109
00:07:48,054 --> 00:07:53,880
so a release is a controlled, repeatable step rather than copying files by hand.

110
00:07:53,880 --> 00:07:58,200
In conclusion, the Library Usage Monitoring and Statistics System

111
00:07:58,200 --> 00:08:01,922
replaces a paper logbook with a working web application.

112
00:08:01,922 --> 00:08:05,112
Students check in and out from their own phones,

113
00:08:05,112 --> 00:08:08,036
every visit is stored in a central database,

114
00:08:08,036 --> 00:08:11,094
staff can see who is in the library right now,

115
00:08:11,094 --> 00:08:16,145
and the system turns that stored data into statistics and printable reports.

116
00:08:16,145 --> 00:08:20,930
Thank you for your attention. We are now ready to answer your questions.
